# 9. Four Types of Errors

The major errors covered are:

```text
1. Preprocessor Error
2. Compile-Time Error
3. Linker Error
4. Run-Time Error
```

---

# 10. Error 1 — Preprocessor Error

## **Description**

A **preprocessor error** occurs during the preprocessing stage.

It commonly occurs when a required header file cannot be found.

## **Example**

```c
#include <unknown_header.h>

int main()
{
    return 0;
}
```

## **Command**

```bash
gcc -E program.c -o program.i
```

## **Possible Error**

```text
fatal error: unknown_header.h: No such file or directory
```

## **Cause**

The required header file is not available.

---

# 11. Error 2 — Compile-Time Error

## **Description**

A **compile-time error** occurs when the compiler finds invalid C syntax or an invalid C language construct.

## **Example**

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main()
{
    int a = 10

    printf("%d\n", a);

    return 0;
}
```

The semicolon `;` is missing after:

```c
int a = 10
```

## **Command**

```bash
gcc -S program.c -o program.s
```

The compiler reports the error.

## **Correct Code**

```c
int a = 10;
```

---

# 12. Error 3 — Linker Error

## **Description**

A **linker error** occurs when the linker cannot find the definition of a required function or symbol.

## **Example**

```c
int main()
{
    display();

    return 0;
}
```

Here, `display()` is called but its definition is not provided.

## **Command**

```bash
gcc program.c -o program
```

## **Possible Error**

```text
undefined reference to `display'
```

## **Cause**

The linker cannot resolve the required function or symbol.

---

# 13. Error 4 — Run-Time Error

## **Description**

A **run-time error** occurs while the executable program is running.

The program may compile and link successfully but fail during execution.

Common examples include:

```text
Segmentation fault
Floating-point exception
Bus error
```

---

# 14. Run-Time Error Example — Segmentation Fault

## **Program**

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main()
{
    int *p = NULL;

    printf("%d\n", *p);

    return 0;
}
```

## **Compile**

```bash
gcc program.c -o program
```

## **Run**

```bash
./program
```

## **Possible Output**

```text
Segmentation fault
```

---

# 15. Run-Time Error Example — Division by Zero

## **Program**

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main()
{
    int a = 10;
    int b = 0;

    printf("%d\n", a / b);

    return 0;
}
```

## **Compile**

```bash
gcc program.c -o program
```

## **Run**

```bash
./program
```

On many Linux systems, this results in:

```text
Floating point exception
```

